-- title:
--   Cylinders of every depth cover the attractor
-- statement:
--   A self-similar system on the line consists of finitely many maps $f_i(x)=r_ix+b_i$ with $0<|r_i|<1$ and weights $w_i>0$ summing to $1$; a probability measure $\mu$ is self-similar when $\mu=\sum_iw_i(f_i)_*\mu$. The coding map sends an address $\omega\in\iota^{\mathbb N}$ to $\pi(\omega)=\lim_nf_{\omega_0}\circ\cdots\circ f_{\omega_{n-1}}(0)$, and the system has no exact overlaps when distinct finite words give distinct composed maps. Let $K$ be the set of all coding limits. For every $n$,
--   $$
--   K\subseteq\bigcup_{|w|=n}f_w(K),
--   $$
--   where $f_w$ is the composed map of the word $w$.
--
--   These covers are used to bound the Hausdorff measure of the attractor.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/OAI/MeasureTheory/SelfSimilar/Attractor.lean#L102-L120

